flow "K-Means clustering"
summary "Alternates nearest-centroid assignment and mean recomputation to minimise within-cluster inertia, and traps in poor local minima when initial centroids start poorly."
tags clustering unsupervised k-means
level beginner
source "https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/clustering.html#k-means"
source "https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.cluster.KMeans.html"
related machine-learning/gradient-descent

layout direction=LR

node init "Choose K centroids" service
node assign "Assign nearest" service tone=primary
node update "Recompute means" service
node check "Centroids moved?" decision
node done "Final clusters" state

edge init > assign
edge assign > update
edge update > check
edge check > assign dashed
edge check > done

array pts "Points (K = 3)" = 0 1 10 11 20 21
array meta "Centroids & inertia" keys=c1,c2,c3,inertia = – – – –

scenario lloyd "k-means++ init reaches global optimum"
  step seed "k-means++ spreads initial centroids"
    "Six 1D points form three natural pairs (`{0,1}`, `{10,11}`, `{20,21}`). **k-means++** picks centroids far apart (`c1 = 0`, `c2 = 10`, `c3 = 21`) instead of clustering them together."
    detail "k-means++ picks the first centroid uniformly at random, then samples each subsequent centroid with probability proportional to its squared distance from the nearest existing centroid—making well-separated seeds far more likely."
    state init active
    label meta#c1 "0.0"
    label meta#c2 "10.0" with
    label meta#c3 "21.0" with
    callout init "Well-separated initial seeds"
  step assign-step "Assign each point to its nearest centroid"
    "Lloyd's assignment step maps every point to its closest centroid: `{0, 1} → c1`, `{10, 11} → c2`, and `{20, 21} → c3`."
    send init > assign "c1=0, c2=10, c3=21"
    state assign active
    state pts#0..1 active
    state pts#2..3 warning
    state pts#4..5 success
  step update-step "Recompute each centroid as cluster mean"
    "Each centroid moves to the arithmetic mean of its assigned points: `c1 = 0.5`, `c2 = 10.5`, `c3 = 20.5`. Total squared distance (**inertia**) drops to **1.5**."
    send assign > update "3 clusters"
    label meta#c1 "0.5"
    label meta#c2 "10.5" with
    label meta#c3 "20.5" with
    label meta#inertia "1.5" with
    state update active
  step converge "No assignments change: converged!"
    "Re-checking distances yields identical assignments, so Lloyd's algorithm terminates cleanly at the global optimum (`inertia = 1.5`)."
    send update > check "Stable means"
    send check > done "Converged"
    state init,assign,update,check,done success
    callout done "Optimal inertia = 1.5"

scenario bad-init "Bad random init traps in local minimum" from=lloyd/seed
  step bad-seed "Random init places two seeds in one cluster"
    "Naive random seeding picks `c1 = 0`, `c2 = 1`, and `c3 = 11`—two centroids land inside `{0, 1}`, leaving only one centroid for both `{10, 11}` and `{20, 21}`."
    label meta#c1 "0.0"
    label meta#c2 "1.0" with
    label meta#c3 "11.0" with
    state init warning
    callout init "c1 & c2 collide in left pair!"
  step bad-assign "Right two clusters merge under c3"
    "Nearest-centroid assignment puts `0 → c1`, `1 → c2`, and forces all four right points (`10, 11, 20, 21`) into `c3` because `10` is closer to `11` than to `1`."
    send init > assign "c1=0, c2=1, c3=11"
    state pts#0 active
    state pts#1 warning
    state pts#2..5 error
    state assign warning
  step local-min "Trapped in fixed-point local minimum"
    "Updating means yields `c1 = 0.0`, `c2 = 1.0`, `c3 = 15.5` (`inertia = **101.0**`, 67× worse!). Because `10` is still closer to `15.5` than to `1.0`, nothing moves—Lloyd's stops trapped!"
    detail "K-Means inertia is non-convex, so Lloyd's algorithm guarantees convergence only to a local minimum, not the global one. Using `init='k-means++'` and multiple restarts (`n_init`) lets scikit-learn keep the run with lowest inertia."
    send assign > update "Skewed clusters"
    send update > check "0 moves"
    send check > done "Stuck!"
    label meta#c1 "0.0"
    label meta#c2 "1.0" with
    label meta#c3 "15.5" with
    label meta#inertia "101.0" with
    state update,check,done error
    effect done shake
    callout done "Trapped: inertia 101.0 vs 1.5"
